  "response": "Storage-iron assessment is handled with equal care. Serum ferritin is measured together with C-reactive protein so that genuine iron loading can be separated from inflammatory elevation, and serum ferritin concentration is a direct quantitative measure of hepatic iron concentration; the need to pair ferritin with an inflammatory marker rules out treating the ferritin value as such a direct measure of liver iron. By contrast with any inference drawn from a ferritin value, MRI with R2* or T2* relaxometry quantifies hepatic iron concentration without tissue sampling, and it is this imaging estimate that anchors the decision to proceed to molecular testing.\n\nOnce biochemistry points to primary loading, the investigation moves from phenotype to genotype. Because C282Y homozygosity is the most common genotype underlying hereditary haemochromatosis in people of northern European ancestry, HFE genotyping tests specifically for the C282Y and H63D variants.\n\nThe genotyping result then partitions the differential sharply. Either the patient's HFE genotype included at least one copy of the C282Y variant, or the genotype included no copy of the C282Y variant; one of these outcomes must obtain and both cannot, and the subsequent reasoning follows whichever one the laboratory reports.\n\nIn the case under discussion, the patient was homozygous for the C282Y variant; that is, the patient inherited two copies of the C282Y variant, one from each parent. The presence of two copies, one contributed by each parent, shows that hereditary haemochromatosis follows an autosomal recessive inheritance pattern, since affected status requires a variant allele from both sides of the pedigree rather than a single transmitted copy. Hereditary haemochromatosis is transmitted in this autosomal recessive fashion; the patient, by contrast, received repeated red cell transfusions over several years, an iron burden delivered wholly from outside the genome. At least one of two external exposures is therefore documented in this history, and perhaps both: the transfusion course described, or the patient's consumption of high-dose oral iron supplements over several years. Quantifying the transfused iron load and the supplemental intake against the imaging estimate of hepatic iron is what finally apportions the overload between inherited and acquired causes.\n\nThe first branch point in such a workup is biochemical. Transferrin saturation above 45 percent is a standard threshold for suspecting iron overload, while a transferrin saturation below 20 percent confirms a diagnosis of hereditary haemochromatosis; these two cut-offs cannot both be true, and the clinician must settle which one actually governs the interpretation of the patient's iron studies.",
